# Machine-generated tracker exports and how they merge.
#
# The deciduous docs exports are DERIVED artifacts with a mergeable source
# of truth elsewhere: deciduous syncs multi-user/multi-worktree history
# through git-merged event files (`deciduous events rebuild`), and the
# pre-commit hook regenerates these JSONs from the rebuilt database on
# every commit. So at merge time the branch's own copy is always an
# acceptable resolution — whichever side git picked would be overwritten
# by the next commit's fresh export anyway. The `keep-ours` custom driver
# (wired per-clone by scripts/install-hooks.sh:
# `git config merge.keep-ours.driver true`) keeps the current branch's
# version and ends the guaranteed every-PR conflict on these files.
# Note: custom drivers only run in local merges — GitHub's UI can't use
# them, so update branches locally when these conflict.
docs/graph-data.json merge=keep-ours
docs/git-history.json merge=keep-ours

# .chainlink/issues-export.json deliberately gets NO merge driver
# (data-integrity review, PR #9). Unlike deciduous, chainlink has no
# tracked event log: this export is the ONLY git-visible carrier of issue
# records, and linked worktrees / cloud sandboxes each have their own
# untracked issues.db — so two branches genuinely can hold disjoint
# record sets, and keep-ours would silently discard one side before it
# was ever imported. A conflict on this file is signal, not noise.
# To resolve one: `git checkout --theirs .chainlink/issues-export.json`,
# then `chainlink import .chainlink/issues-export.json` (folds the other
# side's records into the local db), then commit — the pre-commit hook
# regenerates a true union export from the merged database.

# Chainlink appends an entry on every session end, on whatever branch is
# checked out; two branches touching the changelog is the normal case,
# not a conflict. The built-in union driver keeps both sides' lines.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
